      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Rimini Mining District sits 13 miles west of Helena, east of the Continental Divide, at the end of a Northern Pacific Railroad branch.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rimini%2C_Montana",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rimini still hosts several historic structures, including the 1904 Rimini School‑Community Center, both restored and listed on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoveringmontana.com/ghost-towns/rimini/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The EPA designates Rimini as part of the Upper Tenmile Creek Superfund area, prioritizing 70 of 150 mine sites for cleanup.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rimini%2C_Montana",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Silver lodes discovered in 1864 sparked Rimini’s founding, making it one of Montana’s oldest lead‑zinc mining camps.",
